#Problem Statement

Human–elephant conflict (HEC) is a major challenge in areas where forests border agricultural lands and villages. Due to habitat loss, deforestation, and limited food and water resources, elephants frequently enter human settlements, causing crop damage, property loss, economic hardship, and risks to both human and elephant lives.

Existing monitoring methods such as manual patrolling, electric fencing, watchtowers, and conventional CCTV systems are expensive, labor-intensive, and often fail to provide timely warnings. Many AI-based surveillance systems also rely on continuous video processing, cloud connectivity, and cellular networks, leading to high power consumption and poor suitability for remote forest areas.

To address these limitations, an **AI-Based Elephant Intrusion Detection System** is proposed. The system integrates **Edge AI** for real-time elephant detection, **PIR sensors** for motion-based camera activation, **LoRaWAN** for long-range communication without cellular networks, and **solar power** for continuous operation in remote locations.

The proposed solution provides **early intrusion detection, instant alerts, low-power operation, reduced maintenance, and reliable monitoring**, helping forest authorities and nearby communities minimize human–elephant conflict, reduce crop damage, protect lives, and support wildlife conservation.
